n definition and the conditions provided in the problem.\n\nFirst, we use the operation definition \\(x \\star y = ax + by + cxy\\) and apply it to the given conditions.\n\n1. From \\(1 \\star 2 = 3\\), we have:\n\\[\n1 \\star 2 = a(1) + b(2) + c(1)(2) = a + 2b + 2c = 3.\n\\]\nThis gives us the equation:\n\\[\na + 2b + 2c = 3. \\quad \\text{(Equation 1)}\n\\]\n\n2. From \\(2 \\star 3 = 4\\), we have:\n\\[\n2 \\star 3 = a(2) + b(3) + c(2)(3) = 2a + 3b + 6c = 4.\n\\]\nThis gives us the equation:\n\\[\n2a + 3b + 6c = 4. \\quad \\text{(Equation 2)}\n\\]\n\n3. We are also given that there is a non-zero real number \\(d\\) such that \\(x \\star d = x\\) for any real number \\(x\\). This implies:\n\\[\nx \\star d = ax + bd + cxd = x.\n\\]\nRearranging terms, we get:\n\\[\nax + bd + cxd = x \\implies ax + cxd = x - bd \\implies x(a + cd) = x - bd.\n\\]\nSince this must hold for any real number \\(x\\), we can equate the coefficients of \\(x\\) on both sides:\n\\[\na + cd = 1 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad -bd = 0.\n\\]\nSince \\(d\\) is a non-zero real number, we have:\n\\[\nb = 0.\n\\]\nSubstituting \\(b = 0\\) into Equations 1 and 2, we get:\n\\[\na + 2c = 3, \\quad \\text{(Equation 1')}\n\\]\n\\[\n2a + 6c = 4. \\quad \\text{(Equation 2')}\n\\]\n\nWe can solve these two equations simultaneously. First, we multiply Equation 1' by 2:\n\\[\n2a + 4c = 6. \\quad \\text{(Equation 3)}\n\\]\nNow, we subtract Equation 2' from Equation 3:\n\\[\n(2a + 4c) - (2a + 6c) = 6 - 4 \\implies -2c = 2 \\implies c = -1.\n\\]\nSubstituting \\(c = -1\\) back into Equation 1':\n\\[\na + 2(-1) = 3 \\implies a - 2 = 3 \\implies a = 5.\n\\]\nNow we have \\(a = 5\\), \\(b = 0\\), and \\(c = -1\\). We substitute these values into the equation \\(a + cd = 1\\):\n\\[\n5 + (-1)d = 1 \\implies 5 - d = 1 \\implies d = 4.\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(d\\) is \\(\\boxed{4}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{4}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
